# Commands and environment

Request this rule when running shell commands, installing packages, or writing run instructions.

## Before a command

1. Detect the OS. This workspace is often Windows PowerShell. Do not assume `cp`, `source`, or bash heredocs work.
2. Prefer `python -m ...` so the module comes from the active interpreter.
3. Use the repo virtualenv if it exists (`.venv/`). Do not install packages into the global interpreter.
4. Do not add a dependency until the user agrees. Do not create `requirements.txt` or `pyproject.toml` as a side effect.

Windows venv examples:

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe src\notebooks\00_verify_and_sample.py
```

## Commands that exist

| Command | Status |
|---|---|
| `python src/notebooks/00_verify_and_sample.py` | Exists. Optional `--orders` and `--seed` (defaults 5000 and 42). |
| Copy `.env.example` to `.env` | Documented. On PowerShell use `Copy-Item .env.example .env`. |
| Databricks job / Volume upload | TBD until spikes S1 and S4 record the real steps. |
| Local `pyspark` + `delta-spark` on `data/sample/` | TBD until spike S6. |
| `python -m pytest` | Tooling is planned (`src/tests/`). No test module exists yet — do not invent a pytest invocation that imports missing code. |

If a command is not in this table and not in `README.md`, mark it TBD in the docs. Do not fabricate a CLI.
